          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "d7aadd7b-c79d-53c6-8510-2c6d241742b9"
          ]
        },
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "f98e68fd-f16f-512b-a9c0-e68c60cf27b0",
          ":answer-field/key": "III",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "39403a60-d7b0-5d33-a9b5-7b90ea4723be",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/value": "false"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "78579b73-4f9b-591d-bd7b-032c0e529554",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/value": "true"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "39403a60-d7b0-5d33-a9b5-7b90ea4723be"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ therefore the function doesn't have a maximum value. Hence, statements I and II are both false.\n\n- The function's domain is all real numbers except the integer multiples of $\\pi $, such as $0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,….$ Hence, statement III is false.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
    },
